{"id": "sevt_018goZsyCyEM81beKaQb1q6p", "input": {"evidence": "tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:10 builds an INSERT by string concatenation, but the only values used are `name`/`email` from the loop at tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:9. That loop goes over the module-level constant USERS, defined at tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:3 as two hardcoded tuples (\"alice\"/\"bob\" plus example.com emails). The function seed(path) at tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:6 takes only a DB file path, and that path goes only to sqlite3.connect at tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:7, not into any SQL text. No user input reaches the query. Reachability: a repo-wide search for seed_db/seed/fixtures found no callers apart from the definition at tests/fixtures/seed_db.py:6. The only application module, app/main.py:1-8, imports nothing but Flask and has just a /health route (app/main.py:6-8) that returns a static dict. config.py:1 imports only os and does not refer to the fixture. So the code is test-only, cannot be reached from any HTTP entry point, and handles no attacker-controlled data.", "finding_id": "F-105", "recommended_fix": "No security fix is needed. As a hygiene change, and to stop the scanner from flagging this again, use parameterized queries: conn.execute(\"INSERT INTO users VALUES (?, ?)\", (name, email)) or conn.executemany(...) with USERS. You could also exclude tests/ from the production SAST scope or suppress this finding with a justification.", "severity": "none", "title": "False positive: string-built INSERT in test fixture uses only hardcoded constants", "verdict": "not_exploitable"}, "name": "create_ticket_draft", "processed_at": "2026-10-26T20:54:32.695143Z", "type": "agent.custom_tool_use"}
